*Context: *The Around the Horn vote was overwhelmingly in favor of the
China Academy of Information and Communications Technology research report
on AI safety/security governance (link to *original Chinese
<https://substack.com/redirect/a1f233d7-6853-4fa7-a7b0-56b047a69c0a?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
).=C2=B9 I can always trust my readers to vote for the nerdy white paper, e=
ven
though I was cheering for the article titled =E2=80=9CThe People who Resist=
 AI=E2=80=9D
(perhaps I=E2=80=99ll cover that one in a future issue).

*Key Takeaways: Chinese scholars and policymakers are very plugged into
developments in global AI governance. The report=E2=80=99s four authors are
affiliated with CAICT, the China AI Industry Alliance, the Ministry of
Information and Industry Technology, and the Ministry of Public Security. *=
A
few examples that illustrate this knowledge diffusion:

   -

   In discussing new AI security risks, the report cites the
   =E2=80=9CJailbreakRadar=E2=80=9D working paper (image below), published =
by researchers from
   CISPA Helmholtz Center for Information Security, a leading cybersecurity
   research center based in Germany.

<https://substack.com/redirect/617a6430-e8d7-4104-9ed8-e967f0027100?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>
Source:
https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.05668

   -

   They also cite the Open Worldwide Application Security Project=E2=80=99s=
 list of
   ten critical vulnerabilities for large language models. This stood out t=
o
   me because a Tencent Research Institute report on large model security a=
nd
   ethics also highlighted this same list (*ChinAI #254
   <https://substack.com/redirect/e771f6b9-5e3a-4394-bb70-a4c5936d76b8?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
   )
   -

   Interestingly, the report highlights fast-developing model capabilities,
   which raise =E2=80=9Cunknown risks=E2=80=9D including: =E2=80=9Cvalue al=
ignment lapses=E2=80=9D [=E4=BB=B7=E5=80=BC=E5=AF=B9=E9=BD=90=E5=A4=B1=E6=
=95=88].
   In this section, the authors also cite the Stanford AI Index Report, whi=
ch
   benchmarks AI capabilities against human performance.

*This report is also a useful resource to keep up to date with AI
governance events. *Admittedly, I do not really keep up with all the latest
breaking news in this area, but I would consider myself generally aware
=E2=80=94and I learned a lot from digesting the report. A few developments =
that
caught my eye:

   -

   On slide 6, there was a very intriguing reference to a chess game (CAICT
   report says Chinese chess [xiangqi], but I think that=E2=80=99s an error=
) that
   occurred between DeepSeek and ChatGPT. Apparently, in February 2025,
   DeepSeek and ChatGPT played a game, in which DeepSeek tricked ChatGPT in=
to
   believing that the rules of chess had been updated, and after making a
   bunch of illegal moves, eventually won the game.=C2=B2
   -

   On the slide that discusses key challenges in AI safety/security, the
   report highlights how open-source ecosystems lower the barrier to
misuse: =E2=80=9C*In
   September 2025, KawaiiGPT and other black market tools were spread on
   open-source community Github. They could also be deployed on the Linux
   system in under five minutes*.=E2=80=9D
   -

      However, according to this analysis of Dark LLMs like KawaiiGPT: =E2=
=80=9C*For
      all of the help they provide to low-level hackers, what also stands o=
ut
      about WormGPT 4 and KawaiiGPT is just how technically underwhelming t=
hey
      are, at least compared to popular predictions about AI malware
in the media*
      .=E2=80=9D
      -

   Lastly, the report cites a fascinating statistic: =E2=80=9C*In February =
2025,
   Jinri Toutiao (Chinese news platform) published a 2024 governance report
   with data that showed: the platform blocked over 930,000 pieces of
   low-quality AI-generated content; over 7.81 million instances of repetit=
ive
   or identical posts were penalized*.=E2=80=9D The authors frame this as a=
n
   information pollution risk, at the societal level.

*One thing to keep tracking is CAICT=E2=80=99s AI Safety benchmark*. When I
conducted a check-in of three key AI safety benchmarks maintained by
Chinese organizations, this was the only one that seemed to follow through
with regular updates (*ChinAI #315
<https://substack.com/redirect/3001a0a9-bd1b-4735-856f-18aa85e8e144?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*).
However, I noted:

One trend that stood out is the shrinking scope of their updates, at least
compared to the relatively comprehensive coverage of the initial safety
benchmark from April 2024 =E2=80=94 which included topics such as =E2=80=9C=
*Appeal for
power=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9CInclination for anti-humanity.=E2=80=9D *For in=
stance, the *2025 Q1
benchmark
<https://substack.com/redirect/4cc2b270-4ab6-4b30-9365-39c33a0b066a?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>update*,
published May 8, 2025, exclusively reports results related to hallucination=
.


   -

   The report=E2=80=99s only reference to the CAICT safety benchmark, at le=
ast from
   what I=E2=80=99ve read so far, is to the hallucination evaluations (resu=
lts below).
   All fifteen models exhibited a hallucination rate of over 10 percent,
   though there was a wide range.

